      "record": {
        "knowledge_point": {
          "db_id": 17592186100869,
          "id": "b494140f-206a-5317-8079-ff719a253140",
          "title": "Determining Whether Two Matrices Are Conformable for Multiplication"
        },
        "question": {
          ":question/id": "627f694a-ee30-5a97-a0e2-84f88a4e20d8",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
          ":question/problem": "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -9 & 9 & -9 \\\\ 9 & -9 & 9 & -9 \\\\ 9 & -9 & 9 & -9 \\\\ 9 & -9 & 9 & -9\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -9 \\\\ 9 & -9 \\\\ 9 & -9 \\\\ 9 & -9\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -9 & 9 & -9 \\\\ 9 & -9 & 9 & -9\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the matrices $A,B,$ and $C?$\n\n- **I.** The product $AB$ is well defined\n- **II.** The product $BA$ is well defined\n- **III.** The product $AC$ is well defined\n\nSelect all that apply.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, $B$ is a $4\\times 2$ matrix, and $C$ is a $2\\times 4$ matrix.\n\nThere are $4$ columns in $A$ and $4$ rows in $B.$ Since the number of columns in $A$ equals the number of rows in $B,$ the matrices $A$ and $B$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{A}_{4\\times \\boxed{\\text{4}}}{\\times }_{\u2713}{B}_{\\boxed{\\text{4}}\\times 2}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, there are $2$ columns in $B$ and $4$ rows in $A.$ Since the number of columns in $B$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $A,$ the matrices $B$ and $A$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{B}_{4\\times 2}{\\times }_{\\ne }{A}_{4\\times 4}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nFinally, there are $4$ columns in $A$ and $2$ rows in $C.$ Since the number of columns in $A$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $C,$ the matrices $A$ and $C$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{A}_{4\\times 4}{\\times }_{\\ne }{C}_{2\\times 4}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nTherefore, statement I is true, while statements II and III are false.",
